  [quad_factor_ab__demonstrated__gap__40__expert] (441 tok) "You're on the right track, but there's a small **misconception** in your reasoning.\n\n---\n\n### Let's go through it step by step:\n\nYou correctly **factorized** the equation:\n\n$$\nt^2 - 9 = 0\n$$\n\nas:\n\n$$\n(t - 3)(t + 3) = 0\n$$\n\nThis is the **difference of squares** identity:\n\n$$\na^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b)\n$$\n\nSo, the correct factorization is:\n\n$$\n(t - 3)(t + 3) = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Solving the equation:\n\nFrom the factored form:\n\n$$\n(t - 3)(t + 3) = 0\n$$\n\nWe use the **zero product property**, which says that if the product of two factors is zero, then **at least one** of the factors must be zero.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nt - 3 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad t + 3 = 0\n$$\n\nSolving each:\n\n- $ t - 3 = 0 \\Rightarrow t = 3 $\n- $ t + 3 = 0 \\Rightarrow t = -3 $\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\nt^2 - 9 = 0 \\quad \\text{has two solutions:} \\quad t = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = -3\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n\n- You **correctly** factorized the equation.\n- You **incorrectly** wrote the factorization as $ (t - 3)(t - 3) $, which would imply only one solution.\n- The correct factorization is $ (t - 3)(t + 3) $, leading to **two solutions**.\n\n✅ **Final Answer:**  \n$$\nt^2 - 9 = 0 \\text{ has exactly two solutions: } t = 3 \\text{ and } t = -3\n$$\n\nLet me know if you'd like to explore more about solving quadratic equations!"
